{
 "question": "Where does Dolby's decoder put the clip gain of a JOC payload back, how does it get from one value to the next, and what does doing the same leave between our objects and its?",
 "method": "A JOC payload carries the gain the encoder took off the downmix; the standard gives it a value and no use. `decode --no-clip-gain` writes the objects without it, so the objects Dolby's decoder wrote for the stream over that decode are the gain as Dolby applies it. `tools/joc_clip_gain.py` solves for it by least squares in steps of 16 samples around every change of the gain that stands alone (the same gain in the two frames before and in the frame after, a change of 3 per cent or more), for the LFE and for the objects apart, and where the gain holds it reads the value. The stream is forty seconds of a film in which the gain takes 39 values; Dolby's decoder is run as it stands (`dlbac3dec out-ch-config=21`, `docs/dolby-tools.md`) and only what it writes is read. With the gain put back that way, the 129 tracks of evidence 48 were screened again (`tools/joc_bands.py`, ours with our dither left out), and the corpus was decoded by the binary of main and by the binary of the change (`tools/old_new.py --decode-all`). A track of the library is a number; the films are not in the repository.",
 "commands": [
  "python tools/joc_clip_gain.py clips/joc-clip-gain.ec3 ref-drp/joc/joc-clip-gain.f32 --out gain-dolby.json",
  "python tools/joc_clip_gain.py clips/joc-clip-gain.ec3 --self-check",
  "python tools/joc_bands.py <ours>.atmos.audio <dolby>.f32   (every track, before and after)",
  "python tools/old_new.py --old <main> --new <change> --decode-all <thd> <ec3> <clips> <fixtures>",
  "OADEC_MEDIA=<work> cargo test --release --locked -p oadec-cli --test real -- --ignored the_clip_gain"
 ],
